    user_requests: "Here is the full initial file tree for the application:\n/.env.example\n/.gitignore\n/index.html\n/metadata.json\n/package.json\n/src/App.tsx\n/src/index.css\n/src/main.tsx\n/tsconfig.json\n/vite.config.ts\n\nThe following dependencies are installed:\n@google/genai: ^2.4.0\n@tailwindcss/vite: ^4.3.3\n@vitejs/plugin-react: ^6.1.1\ndotenv: ^17.2.3\nexpress: ^4.21.2\nlucide-react: ^0.546.0\nmotion: ^12.23.24\nreact: ^19.0.1\nreact-dom: ^19.0.1\nvite: ^8.3.0\n\nThe following devDependencies are installed:\n@types/express: ^4.17.21\n@types/node: ^22.14.0\n@types/react: ^19.3.0\n@types/react-dom: ^19.3.0\nautoprefixer: ^10.4.21\nesbuild: ^0.25.0\ntailwindcss: ^4.3.3\ntsx: ^4.21.0\ntypescript: ^7.0.2\n\nKey file contents:\n\n--- /.env.example ---\n```\n# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.\n# Users configure this via the Secrets panel in the AI Studio UI.\nGEMINI_API_KEY=\"MY_GEMINI_API_KEY\"\n\n# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime with the Cloud Run service URL.\n# Used for self-referential links, OAuth callbacks, and API endpoints.\nAPP_URL=\"MY_APP_URL\"\n\n```\n\n--- /index.html ---\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>My Google AI Studio App</title>\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"My Google AI Studio App\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\"root\"></div>\n    <script type=\"module\" src=\"/src/main.tsx\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n```\n\n--- /metadata.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"requestFramePermissions\": [],\n  \"majorCapabilities\": [\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/App.tsx ---\n```typescript\n/**\n * @license\n * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n */\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return <div></div>;\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/index.css ---\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n```\n\n--- /src/main.tsx ---\n```typescript\nimport {createRoot} from \'react-dom/client\';\nimport App from \'./App.tsx\';\nimport \'./index.css\';\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\'root\')!).render(<App />);\n\n```\n\n--- /tsconfig.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"experimentalDecorators\": true,\n    \"useDefineForClassFields\": false,\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"types\": [\"vite/client\"],\n    \"lib\": [\n      \"ES2022\",\n      \"DOM\",\n      \"DOM.Iterable\"\n    ],\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"isolatedModules\": true,\n    \"moduleDetection\": \"force\",\n    \"allowJs\": true,\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"@/*\": [\n        \"./*\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true,\n    \"noEmit\": true\n  },\n  \"exclude\": [\"dist\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /vite.config.ts ---\n```typescript\nimport tailwindcss from \'@tailwindcss/vite\';\nimport react from \'@vitejs/plugin-react\';\nimport path from \'path\';\nimport {defineConfig} from \'vite\';\n\nexport default defineConfig(() => {\n  return {\n    plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],\n    resolve: {\n      alias: {\n        \'@\': path.resolve(__dirname, \'.\'),\n      },\n    },\n    server: {\n      // HMR is disabled in AI Studio via DISABLE_HMR env var.\n      // Do not modify\342\200\224file watching is disabled to prevent flickering during agent edits.\n      hmr: process.env.DISABLE_HMR !== \'true\',\n      // Disable file watching when DISABLE_HMR is true to save CPU during agent edits.\n      watch: process.env.DISABLE_HMR === \'true\' ? null : {},\n    },\n  };\n});\n\n```"
    user_requests: "PROJECT: \"Bhasha Setu\" \342\200\223 an animated, mother-tongue learning app for tribal children in Jharkhand primary schools (Class 1 to 5), supporting SDG 4: Quality Education.\n\nLANGUAGES (ALL THREE REQUIRED)\n1. Santali \342\200\223 Ol Chiki script (Unicode), with Roman transliteration.\n2. Ho \342\200\223 Warang Chiti script (Unicode), with Devanagari and Roman transliteration, since many Ho speakers write in Devanagari.\n3. Mundari \342\200\223 default Devanagari, with Roman and Mundari Bani as optional scripts.\n- The child picks their mother tongue on first launch, and can switch language at any time from the top bar. The app remembers the choice.\n- Every piece of content (titles, lessons, audio, game text, chatbot replies, button labels) is stored per language, so all three languages work identically.\n- Each language has its own script setting, font, audio set and review status. Include Unicode fonts that render Ol Chiki and Warang Chiti correctly.\n- Design the data so more languages (Kurukh, Khortha, Nagpuri and others) can be added later without code changes.\n- Hindi and English are always available as bridge languages, shown on a toggle next to the mother tongue so children transition gradually.\n\nGOAL\nChildren study the official Jharkhand (JCERT) government textbooks in their mother tongue. Each chapter is explained through text, audio, videos, animations and games. The app must feel like a children\'s cartoon world, not a school website.\n\nTARGET USERS\n- Children aged 6 to 11 in Classes 1 to 5, many of whom cannot read well yet. Everything must work through pictures, sound and touch.\n- Teachers, who review and correct translations.\n- Devices: low-cost Android phones and tablets, often with poor or no internet.\n\nCURRICULUM (VERY IMPORTANT)\n- Use ONLY the official JCERT / Jharkhand government textbooks for Classes 1 to 5, all subjects, all chapters. Source: https://jcert.jharkhand.gov.in/home/textbook\n- Do NOT invent, guess or paraphrase chapter titles. Chapter names, numbers and order must match the books exactly.\n- Store all curriculum in one data file (JSON or database) with this structure: class, subject, book name, chapter number, original title (Hindi/English), then for each language (santali, ho, mundari): title, script text, transliteration, lesson content, audio file, video link, game data, review status.\n- Provide an import script or admin page that loads chapter lists and content from this data file. I will supply the chapter lists and PDFs.\n- Adding a class, subject, chapter or language must need only a data change, never a code change.\n\nUSER FLOW\n1. Welcome screen: an animated mascot greets the child, with audio. First choice: \"Which is your language?\" with three large buttons (Santali, Ho, Mundari), each with its own script and audio greeting.\n2. Choose class (1 to 5): big colourful animal-themed buttons.\n3. Choose subject: large icon tiles with sound on tap.\n4. Choose chapter: a visual path or map with numbered chapter cards and pictures. Completed chapters show stars.\n5. Open chapter: a lesson screen with these parts:\n   a. Explanation of the chapter in the chosen language, showing the native script, transliteration, and a toggle for the Hindi/English original.\n   b. A Play button for audio narration. Words highlight as they are spoken.\n   c. An animated, illustrated explanation (cartoon scenes, moving characters, walking animals, visuals for each concept).\n   d. A short video for the chapter in that language.\n   e. Interactive games for the chapter: drag and drop, matching, counting, quizzes, sorting, puzzles, tracing letters.\n   f. An \"Ask Chotu\" button that opens the chatbot.\n6. Rewards: stars, badges, a sticker collection, and a celebration with confetti and sound after each game.\n\nCHATBOT (\"Chotu\")\n- A friendly cartoon character that children can type to or speak to.\n- Knows the current class, subject and chapter, and answers doubts about that chapter in very simple sentences.\n- Replies in the child\'s chosen language (Santali, Ho or Mundari), or in Hindi/English if the child uses those, with audio playback.\n- Must say so honestly if it is not sure of a word, and never invent facts. Ho and Mundari have far less AI support than Santali, so Chotu should fall back to Hindi/English plus whichever words it is confident about.\n- Child-safe tone; refuses off-topic or unsafe requests.\n\nUI AND ANIMATION REQUIREMENTS\n- Fully animated, bright, colourful and playful: bouncing buttons, floating clouds, walking animals and children, a dancing mascot, smooth page transitions, confetti.\n- Very large touch targets, minimal text, an icon plus audio for every button, and a high-contrast mode.\n- Large, readable fonts for Ol Chiki, Warang Chiti and Devanagari.\n- Works in portrait and landscape, on small phones and tablets.\n- Light and dark mode. Respect a \"reduce motion\" setting.\n\nTRANSLATION, AUDIO AND QUALITY CONTROL\n- Translation: Hindi/English to Santali, Ho and Mundari using an AI translation service, a school-term glossary per language, and optional Bhashini / AI4Bharat integration.\n- Resource levels differ: Santali has the most digital support, while Ho and Mundari have very little. Start with glossary-based and human-written content for Ho and Mundari, and use AI only to draft.\n- Audio: use pre-recorded native-speaker audio per chapter and per language. If recordings are missing, use text-to-speech only as a clearly marked placeholder, or hide the audio button.\n- Every piece of text carries a review status per language: \"AI draft\", \"teacher-reviewed\" or \"approved\". Show a small badge. Never present unreviewed text as final.\n- Provide a \"Report wrong translation\" button on every screen that sends feedback to that language\'s review queue.\n\nTEACHER / ADMIN PANEL\n- Teacher login, with each teacher assigned to one or more languages.\n- Review queue per language: see AI drafts, edit, approve or reject, and upload native-speaker audio.\n- Upload videos, images and game content per chapter and language.\n- Dashboard: chapters completed, game scores and common wrong answers per class and per language.\n- Export approved translations as a dataset to improve the translation models over time.\n\nTECH REQUIREMENTS\n- Progressive Web App (installable, works offline). Cache downloaded chapters, audio and videos per language. Sync progress when the internet returns.\n- Frontend: React (or similar) with Tailwind, and Lottie/CSS animations. Lightweight, so it runs smoothly on low-end Android phones.\n- Backend: a simple database (Supabase or Firebase) for content, progress, feedback and teacher accounts.\n- Respect child privacy: no ads, no tracking, and minimal personal data (a first name or avatar only).\n\nBUILD ORDER\n1. Project setup, theme, mascot, language switcher and the class > subject > chapter navigation, driven by the curriculum data file.\n2. Lesson screen: text, audio player and animated explanation for all three languages.\n3. Game engine: reusable game templates that take chapter data in any language.\n4. Chatbot (Chotu).\n5. Rewards and progress tracking.\n6. Teacher panel and per-language review workflow.\n7. Offline mode (PWA).\n8. Fully complete ONE chapter (Class 1 Maths, Chapter 1) end to end in all three languages as the quality benchmark, then scale to all chapters and classes.\n\nQUALITY RULES\n- Never make up curriculum or translations. If a chapter or language version is missing, show a \"Coming soon\" tile instead of placeholder text.\n- Keep the code clean, modular and commented so non-experts can add chapters and languages.\n- Test on a small Android phone screen first.\n\nDELIVERABLES\nWorking app, the curriculum data file with its schema, a README explaining how to add chapters, audio, games and languages, and a short guide for teachers."
