  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Exploring Class Options**\n\nI\'m currently focused on how selecting a class and subject impacts the available materials. For instance, choosing Class 1 reveals options like \"\340\244\260\340\245\213\340\244\232\340\244\225 \340\244\227\340\244\243\340\244\277\340\244\244 - 1\" for Maths, \"\340\244\235\340\244\277\340\244\262\340\244\256\340\244\277\340\244\262 - 1\" for Hindi, and \"Sunrise - 1\" for English, each with specific chapter or unit listings. My goal is to grasp the hierarchical relationship between class, subject, and associated learning resources.\n\n\n**Expanding Class Analysis**\n\nI\'ve moved on to analyzing Class 2 and Class 3 now. I\'m noting the specific subjects like Maths, Hindi, and English, along with their associated chapters and units. The goal is to see how the subject content evolves and to identify patterns and relationships across the different classes. The JCERT designations are really helping to provide useful constraints, and the official chapters or units are providing some real, concrete context.\n\n\n**Analyzing Class Content**\n\nI am now closely examining the content for Class 1, looking into the specific chapters for subjects like Hindi, and Environmental Science (EVS). The chapter titles themselves are offering a fresh perspective. I am particularly interested in how themes like animals, water, and community are introduced to this age group. My objective is to build a clearer picture of the subject matter and learning objectives for this class level.\n\n\n**Examining Class Resources**\n\nI\'m now diving into the resources available for Class 4. In Maths, the \"\340\244\260\340\245\213\340\244\232\340\244\225 \340\244\227\340\244\243\340\244\277\340\244\244 - 4\" has twelve chapters covering geometry, numbers, operations, fractions, time, measurement, and data representation. \"\340\244\255\340\244\276\340\244\267\340\244\276\340\244\202\340\244\234\340\244\262\340\244\277 - 4\" for Hindi has thirteen chapters, including poems and stories. English \"Sunshine - 4\" has ten units covering a range of topics. I\'m noting the variety of themes and the structure of the learning materials.\n\n\n**Investigating Chapter Structures**\n\nI\'m now focusing on Class 2 EVS (Environmental Science) to compare to earlier analyses. \"\340\244\271\340\244\256\340\244\276\340\244\260\340\245\200 \340\244\246\340\245\201\340\244\250\340\244\277\340\244\257\340\244\276 - 2\" contains 10 chapters, which cover a range of practical topics, such as family, community, animals, and journeys. My goal is to use this additional data to better understand curriculum development. The progression of themes from Class 1 to Class 2 is helpful.\n\n\n**Comparing Class Five Materials**\n\nI\'m now examining Class 5 in more detail, noting that Hindi has twenty chapters covering a diverse range of topics, stories, and poems. In comparison, English \'Sunshine - 5\' has ten units, and EVS (Environmental Science) \'\340\244\271\340\244\256\340\244\276\340\244\260\340\245\200 \340\244\246\340\245\201\340\244\250\340\244\277\340\244\257\340\244\276 - 3\' has several chapters to examine. I\'m cross-referencing this with the earlier Class analyses to find patterns in the development of themes across the classes.\n\n\n**Confirming Textbook Alignment**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a set of chapters for a specific class level: \332\251\331\210\331\206 \332\251\330\263 \332\251\333\222 \330\254\333\214\330\263\330\247, \332\251\330\247\331\205 \332\251\333\214 \340\244\226\340\245\213\340\244\234, etc. My focus is on the authentic and precise alignment with the official JCERT Jharkhand textbooks. I\'m thinking about how the `SubjectSelector.tsx` component should dynamically display the exact textbook name and description for each subject based on the selected class. The goal is to provide accurate and relevant learning resource information to the user.\n\n\n**Mapping Class Relationships**\n\nI\'m now mapping out subject offerings across classes. I\'ve noted the consistent JCERT Jharkhand textbook designations for each class level: Class 2 offers \"\340\244\235\340\244\277\340\244\262\340\244\256\340\244\277\340\244\262 - 2\" for Hindi and \"Sunrise - 2\" for English. Class 3 introduces \"\340\244\260\340\245\213\340\244\232\340\244\225 \340\244\227\340\244\243\340\244\277\340\244\244 - 3\", \"\340\244\255\340\244\276\340\244\267\340\244\276\340\244\202\340\244\234\340\244\262\340\244\277 - 3\", \"Sunshine - 3\", and \"\340\244\271\340\244\256\340\244\276\340\244\260\340\245\200 \340\244\246\340\245\201\340\244\250\340\244\277\340\244\257\340\244\276 - 1\". The patterns continue with Class 4 and 5, helping me to understand how resources are structured per subject and grade. This confirms how `SubjectSelector.tsx` will function.\n\n\n**Defining Subject Structures**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the subject structure across classes, given the user feedback on textbook accuracy. I\'ve noted that Classes 1 & 2 use Hindi (\"\340\244\235\340\244\277\340\244\262\340\244\256\340\244\277\340\244\262\"), English (\"Sunrise\"), and Maths (\"\340\244\260\340\245\213\340\244\232\340\244\225 \340\244\227\340\244\243\340\244\277\340\244\244\"). For Classes 3, 4, & 5, we have Hindi (\"\340\244\255\340\244\276\340\244\267\340\244\276\340\244\202\340\244\234\340\244\262\340\244\277\"), English (\"Sunshine\"), and Maths (\"\340\244\260\340\245\213\340\244\232\340\244\225 \340\244\227\340\244\243\340\244\277\340\244\244\"), plus EVS. I\'m confirming that the `SubjectSelector.tsx` component should adapt to these class-specific subject offerings.\n\n\n"
